<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pachacuti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1438–<a href="/years/1471">1471</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Pachacutec, Cusi Yupanqui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pachacuti, born Cusi Yupanqui, was the ninth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a>, ruling from about 1438 to 1471. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> tradition he saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> from the Chanka, deposed his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca">Viracocha Inca</a> and began the conquests that turned the kingdom of Cusco into the Inca Empire. He is credited with rebuilding Cusco and with founding royal estates such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachacuti.jpg" alt="Pachacuti" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Pachacuti,_Tenth_Inca,_1_of_14_Portraits_of_Inca_Kings_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pachacuti was known as Cusi Yupanqui before he took his throne name, which in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> evokes pachakutiy, the turn of the world. According to the Spanish chroniclers he was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> at the palace of Cusicancha and was the son of the eighth ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca">Inca Viracocha</a>. The historian María Rostworowski concluded from Andean customs of succession, which let the most able take power, that he was probably not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha">Viracocha</a>'s son. His brother Inca Urco was the appointed co-ruler and heir. In the early 15th century, in the version most Spanish sources support, the Chankas invaded the Cusco confederation. Viracocha and Urco fled, while Cusi Yupanqui rallied the army and defended the city.</p><p>At the battle of Yahuar Pampa the Chankas were beaten. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha">Viracocha</a> gave the honour of trampling the captives to Urco, Yupanqui refused, and Viracocha went into exile after a failed plot against him. Urco died in a short civil war and Yupanqui was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> as Pachacuti. Since the early 1980s most scholars have treated the Chanka war as largely mythical, following Pierre Duviols, and some regard Pachacuti himself as a representation of the start of imperial expansion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> did not count years systematically, so the dates are approximations. Pachacuti conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> and took Chincha on the south-central coast. His army defeated the Colla at Ayaviri and received the submission of the Lupaca, Pacasa and Azangaro peoples, and his generals pressed north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> chiefdom, bringing tension with the Chimú Empire. Local lords were bound by reciprocal gifts, and women from Cusco were given to chiefs as principal wives so that their children would rule.</p><p>He ordered painted panels to record the history of earlier rulers, and is credited with irrigation networks, terraces, roads and hospices. He moved hundreds of thousands of people to colonise remote edges of the empire. These forced settlers were called mitimaes. Most archaeologists now believe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a> was built as an estate for him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pachacuti named his son Amaru Yupanqui co-ruler, then replaced him with Tupac Yupanqui as lacking military ability. He died in 1471 of a terminal illness. His deeds were kept in genealogical histories and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">quipus</a> near his royal mummy. He is also credited with the Sacred Hymns of the Situa ceremony.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pachacuti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the chronicles, the aged ruler Viracocha Inca abandoned the city, but his son <b>Cusi Yupanqui</b> defended it, defeated the Chanka and took the throne as Pachacuti.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chimor’s great rival proved to be the Incas, whose armies, led by Topa Inca Yupanqui, son of <b>Pachacuti</b>, overcame the kingdom around 1470.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-chimor">Kingdom of Chimor · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the reign of <b>Pachacuti</b>, who by tradition began the empire’s expansion in 1438, the Incas built in less than a century the largest state of the Americas, Tawantinsuyu, stretching some four thousand kilometres from southern Colombia to central Chile.</blockquote><a href="/language/quechua">Quechua · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Earlier models, based on John H. Rowe's reconstruction of the reign of <b>Pachacuti</b> Inca Yupanqui, place the start of building around 1450, ten years after he took power; a 2021 study led by Richard L. Burger, reporting 26 radiocarbon measurements from human remains, concluded that the site was…</blockquote><a href="/work/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Each lineage kept its founder’s mummy, served his shrines and held his palaces in the city and his country estates, such as Machu Picchu, probably <b>Pachacuti</b>’s, worked by retainers who served it for life.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Cusi Yupanqui</b> defeated them, took the name Pachacuti and began the expansion that created the Inca Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/chanka-confederation">Chanka Confederation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-13.52,-71.98&amp;year=1454" aria-label="Cusco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2577 2667 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pachacuti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3027 2937h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-13.52,-71.98&amp;year=1454">What was here in 1454? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
